The minimum viable human team (0–2 employees)
1) CEO — non-delegable
CEO must own:

Opportunity selection (what to build, what to kill)

Customer discovery for the first ~20–30 high-quality interviews (district + homeschool)

Early sales (first pilots / first paying homeschool cohort)

Product direction (what “good” looks like)

**2) One “Project Manager” **
Strong PM instincts + growth ops + basic technical fluency

Runs interview ops, keeps Notion system clean, coordinates contractors, manages funnel experiments, keeps cadence.

What you can outsource almost entirely
A) Engineering (initial build)
Outsourceable: 70–90% early on

Contract a senior full-stack dev (or small studio) for MVP

PM + AI + architect guidance define PRD and acceptance tests

What you keep in-house: product judgment + QA signoff + architecture guardrails (can be AI-assisted)

Warning: Never outsource to a team that cannot ship weekly. You need weekly releases to learn fast.

B) Design
Outsourceable: ~90%

UI/UX designer for 2–3 week bursts (flows + design system)

Brand/landing pages via contractor

C) Content production
Outsourceable: 80–90%

AI drafts; contractor polishes

You approve narrative and claims

D) Lead generation / list building
Outsourceable: 90%

VA + Clay specialist for district contact lists and homeschool community mapping
E) Security / privacy documentation
Outsourceable: 60–80% early

A part-time security consultant to build a “district-ready security packet”

Templates adapted to your architecture

F) Legal
Outsourceable: 100% (but tightly managed)

You provide commercial intent; counsel drafts/reviews NDAs, MSAs, DPAs, ToS/Privacy Policy
What you should NOT outsource (or only later)
1) Core customer interviews (initial 20–30)
You can outsource scheduling, transcription, and synthesis.
You should personally conduct the early conversations because:

You need to hear the language, politics, and real objections

It builds credibility for pilots

Later, you can outsource interviewing to a researcher once your script is mature.

2) Product management (decision authority)
You can outsource PRD drafting, but not prioritization and tradeoffs. That’s the company.

Lean execution model: “Two pods + contractors”
You run two parallel tracks with minimal humans:

Pod 1 — Discovery & Validation (you + AI)
3–5 interviews/week district + 5–10/week homeschool

Weekly scorecard updates

2 experiments/week (landing page, concierge, pricing, outreach)

Pod 2 — Build & Ship (contractors + AI)
Weekly release cadence

You approve acceptance criteria and review demos

Concrete outsourcing plan (what to hire, how many hours)
Here is a realistic configuration that keeps cost and complexity down:

Senior full-stack contractor: 20–30 hrs/week (MVP build)

Product designer: 10 hrs/week for 3–4 weeks, then ad hoc

VA / research assistant: 5–10 hrs/week (scheduling, lists, CRM hygiene)

Security/privacy consultant: 5–10 hrs total to produce “district-ready packet” v1

Attorney: as-needed (DPA/MSA/ToS)

That’s it.

The operating cadence with a minimal human team
Daily (10 min): Orchestrator gives you 3 decisions + 3 actions

Weekly (60 min): Evidence review + decision log update

Weekly (30 min): Contractor demo + next sprint scope

Everything else is asynchronous through the Notion system.

The “outsourcing boundary” rules (hard guardrails)
Contractors deliver working increments weekly, not “big reveals.”

You define acceptance tests (AI can help draft them).

No contractor touches real student PII in early MVP—use synthetic data.

Every major decision gets logged (Decision Log).

Growth experiments must have pass/fail thresholds before launch.
